"64_cocoaui.mst — CocoaUI: the Cocoa GUI's native app shell (CG2 window +
 CG4 protocol/restart + CG5 toolbar/metrics/view-switcher + CG6 Workspace).
 Loaded ONLY by the UI worker via `frontend::world::load_list(vm,
 world/cocoaui.list)`, NEVER from world/world.list — so the CLI, the
 WKWebView GUI, and the base test suite carry none of it (files 63+, the
 conditional layer, §12.3).

 The UI worker VM runs pinned to the process's MAIN thread (AppKit requires
 it). The Rust `cocoa_gui` glue bootstraps NSApplication, boots this VM in
 place, runs `CocoaUI startup` once (this class), then enters [NSApp run]
 with the VM at rest — so every AppKit call below runs INLINE on main (the
 bridge's `onMain` hop is a no-op when the caller already IS main).

 CG5 layout (top to bottom, matching the WKWebView GUI's own shell): a
 TOOLBAR strip (view-switcher buttons left, live VmMetrics readout right),
 a CONTENT HOST that swaps its single subview per the active registered
 view, and a small REVERSE-CHRONOLOGICAL Transcript dock at the bottom
 (newest line first — a log to glance at, not the primary output surface;
 CG6's Workspace Print it is that now). Every subview carries the correct
 NSView autoresizingMask so the window is genuinely resizable (unflipped
 AppKit coordinates: MinYMargin-flexible pins to the TOP, MaxYMargin-
 flexible pins to the BOTTOM, both-sizable fills the middle).

 The toolbar's per-button action is the C6 `macvmAction:` selector
 (`MacvmActionTarget`, CG5): each button mints its OWN action-target ticket
 bound to a tiny `CocoaToolbarAction` block-holder, so N buttons share ONE
 Rust IMP with no per-button Rust code (`objc_delegate.rs`'s existing
 ticket-routing does the rest) — the reusable mechanism this sprint exists
 to prove.

 Icons are the SAME `gui/assets/icons-mono/*.svg` files the WKWebView GUI's
 own toolbar already ships (relative to the process cwd — the repo root,
 matching the `MACVM_WORLD` convention), loaded as `NSImage` template images
 so a future theme's tint color recolors them for free — no duplicate art.

 ON-SCREEN status: the AppKit view construction here is the USER's on-screen
 gate (a `MACVM_COCOA_SNAP` sequence self-verifies before that). The request
 round-trip and (peer,corr) routing are headless-proven (embed.rs CG4
 tests); the action dispatch is headless-proven (cocoa_delegate gate)."

"I hold one block behind a native target/action control. Each toolbar
 button or menu item mints its own action-target delegate on an instance
 of me; when the control fires, macvmAction: runs my block. The delegate
 registry keeps me alive — nothing else needs to hold me."
Object subclass: CocoaToolbarAction [
    "A block-holder so N toolbar buttons can each mint their own C6
     action-target ticket (`MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn:`) while sharing
     ONE Rust IMP (`macvmAction:`). Kept alive by the world-side ticket→
     receiver registry (GC-rooted) — nothing else needs to hold it."
    <classVars: nil>
    | Block |
    CocoaToolbarAction class >> on: aBlock [
        | h | h := self new. h setBlock: aBlock. ^h
    ]
    setBlock: aBlock [ Block := aBlock ]
    macvmAction: sender [ ^Block value ]
]

"CocoaToolbarAction's 1-arg sibling: passes the SENDER through to the block.
 What a gesture-recognizer target needs — the block asks the recognizer
 itself for locationInView: (docs/asset_editors_design.md §5). Same ticket
 discipline: kept alive by the world-side registry, one shared Rust IMP."
Object subclass: CocoaSenderAction [
    <classVars: nil>
    | Block |
    CocoaSenderAction class >> on: aBlock [
        | h | h := self new. h setBlock: aBlock. ^h
    ]
    setBlock: aBlock [ Block := aBlock ]
    macvmAction: sender [ ^Block value: sender ]
]

"I am the native GUI's application shell, running in the UI worker VM on
 the main thread. I build the main window: a toolbar of view-switcher
 buttons plus a live VM metrics readout, a content host whose tabs the
 registered views fill (Workspace, the class browsers, Find, Editor,
 Outliner, Canvas, Help, Debugger), and a newest-first Transcript dock at
 the bottom. My Workspace evaluates the selection — or, with no selection,
 the whole buffer — on the primary VM: Do It reports on the Transcript,
 Print It also splices the result at the caret. I install the menus (File,
 Source, View, Theme, Demos, Debug), the A-/A+ code-font sizing, and
 light/dark theming. Views join me with registerViewNamed:...;
 switchToView: swaps tabs. The primary VM runs off the main thread and is
 reached by message; when it is restarted, environmentRestarted re-syncs
 every view against the fresh environment."
Object subclass: CocoaUI [
    <classVars: Win ContentHost Transcript TranscriptScroll ActTarget
                TranscriptText Views ActiveView ToolbarDelegates
                MetricLabels MemBarFill MemBarWidth Workspace WorkspaceScroll
                WorkspaceTextDelegate TabItems TabIndexes
                TranscriptClearBtn FontDelta
                TranscriptCollapsed TranscriptToggleBtn TranscriptGrain
                TranscriptMenuItem TranscriptSplit TranscriptDock
                JitOn JitMenuItem
                WorkspaceContainer
                HaltOn HaltMenuItem MetricViews
                WinToolbar ToolbarDelegate ToolbarItems MetricsHost
                ToolbarIdentArray ToolbarSpecs ViewItemSpecs
                Theme ThemeMenuItems>

    "The DISPLAY name, macOS house style (macOS/macVM) — not the binary,
     the bundle id, or the MACVM_* environment variables, which stay upper."
    CocoaUI class >> title [ ^'macVM' ]

    "── layout constants ──"
    CocoaUI class >> transcriptHeight [ ^110.0 ]
    CocoaUI class >> iconDir [ ^'gui/assets/icons-mono/' ]
    CocoaUI class >> toolbarTexturePath [ ^'gui/assets/toolbar-texture.png' ]

    "── autoresizing mask bits (NSView) ──"
    CocoaUI class >> maskWidthSizable [ ^2 ]
    CocoaUI class >> maskHeightSizable [ ^16 ]
    CocoaUI class >> maskMinYMargin [ ^8 ]   "flexible space BELOW -> pins to TOP"
    CocoaUI class >> maskMaxYMargin [ ^32 ]  "flexible space ABOVE -> pins to BOTTOM"
    CocoaUI class >> maskMinXMargin [ ^1 ]   "flexible space LEFT -> pins to RIGHT"
    CocoaUI class >> maskMaxXMargin [ ^4 ]   "flexible space RIGHT -> pins to LEFT (fixed width)"

    CocoaUI class >> startup [
        "Build (or rebuild) the window shell and order it front. Returns to
         Rust with the VM at rest; Rust then calls [NSApp run]."
        | winClass content |
        self teardown.
        TranscriptText := ''.
        Views := OrderedCollection new.
        ToolbarDelegates := OrderedCollection new.
        ToolbarSpecs := OrderedCollection new.
        ViewItemSpecs := OrderedCollection new.
        TabItems := OrderedCollection new.
        TabIndexes := Dictionary new.
        winClass := Cocoa classNamed: 'NSWindow'.

        Win := ((winClass onMain alloc) onMain
            "1100x700: the toolbar's own arithmetic sets the floor — the view
             buttons now reach 8 + (11 * 40) = 448px and the metrics cluster
             needs 360 (104 MEM + 4 * 64), so anything under ~824 starts with
             the metrics scrunched under the icons. Extra width also suits the
             4-pane browser and the debugger's stack/source split."
            initWithContentRect: (Array with: 240.0 with: 180.0 with: 1100.0 with: 700.0)
            styleMask: 15 backing: 2 defer: false).
        Win onMain setReleasedWhenClosed: false.
        Win onMain setTitle: self title.
        "NSWindowTitleHidden = 1. In the unified titlebar the title would eat
         ~110pt that the toolbar needs at a normal window width — and a
         single-window app already names itself in the menu bar. The title is
         still SET, so Window-menu entries and Mission Control read right."
        Win onMain setTitleVisibility: 1.
        Win onMain setMinSize: (Array with: 480.0 with: 320.0).
        "Full-screen primary (NSWindowCollectionBehaviorFullScreenPrimary =
         1<<7): without it the green button only zooms, which on a modern Mac
         reads as a broken window rather than a deliberate choice."
        Win onMain setCollectionBehavior: 128.
        "Remember size and position across launches. setFrameAutosaveName:
         registers the key and saves on every move/resize; setFrameUsingName:
         applies whatever was saved (a no-op on first run). Without this the
         window reopens at the hard-coded 1100x700 every time and silently
         discards whatever the user chose — the single most noticeable way an
         app feels un-Mac-like."
        Win onMain setFrameAutosaveName: 'MACVMMainWindow'.
        Win onMain setFrameUsingName: 'MACVMMainWindow'.
        content := Win onMain contentView.
        content onMain setAutoresizesSubviews: true.

        "Read the remembered dock state BEFORE the panes exist, so they can be
         BUILT at the right sizes. Positioning the divider afterwards does not
         work at startup: neither addSubview: nor makeKeyAndOrderFront: lays
         the split view out synchronously, so setPosition: measures against a
         zero-height frame and clamps the dock to nothing."
        self loadTranscriptCollapsed.
        self buildContentHostIn: content.
        self buildTranscriptIn: content.

        self registerViewNamed: #workspace title: 'Workspace' icon: 'texteditor'
             container: [ self workspaceContainer ]
             onShow: [ Win onMain makeFirstResponder: Workspace ].
        "CG7/CG8: later-loading views register themselves — resolved by name
         so this file never depends on files after it in cocoaui.list."
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaBrowser) ifNotNil: [ :bc | bc register ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaBrowser2) ifNotNil: [ :b2 | b2 register ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaFind) ifNotNil: [ :fc | fc register ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaEditor) ifNotNil: [ :ec | ec register ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaOutliner) ifNotNil: [ :oc | oc register ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaCanvas) ifNotNil: [ :cv | cv register ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaHelp) ifNotNil: [ :hc | hc register ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaDebugger) ifNotNil: [ :dc | dc register ].
        "A- / A+ and Do It / Print It, after every view's switcher item."
        self buildFontButtons.
        "Every item is registered now, so the toolbar can ask for the full
         list the moment setToolbar: hands it the delegate."
        self buildWindowToolbar.

        "Forwarded primary transcript lines (§7.4) append to our Transcript view."
        Worker onTranscriptLine: [ :t | CocoaUI appendTranscript: t ].

        self installMenu.
        "After installMenu, so the checkmark lands on a live menu item."
        self applyTheme: self loadTheme.
        self switchToView: #workspace.
        "Apple Event surface (docs/applescript_design.md): replaces the
         minimal cocoa_gui-side app delegate with one that has a door into
         the world. After switchToView:, so a script reading `current view`
         can never observe the pre-registration nil. Absent (a world without
         74_cocoascript.mst) the app simply stays unscriptable."
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaScript) ifNotNil: [ :cs | cs install ].
        Win onMain makeKeyAndOrderFront: nil.
        self appendTranscript: 'CocoaUI: ready.'.
        ^Win
    ]

    "── the real NSToolbar (the titlebar one) ─────────────────────────────
     What AppKit itself draws and manages: it lays the items out, handles
     window resize by moving overflow into the chevron menu, and gives the
     window the standard unified look. Everything it shows is vended by the
     three NSToolbarDelegate callbacks below, driven through a #toolbar-role
     MacvmDelegate (src/runtime/objc_delegate.rs)."

    CocoaUI class >> toolbarIdentifier [ ^'MACVMMainToolbar' ]
    CocoaUI class >> metricsItemIdentifier [ ^'macvm.metrics' ]
    CocoaUI class >> flexibleSpaceIdentifier [ ^'NSToolbarFlexibleSpaceItem' ]

    CocoaUI class >> viewGroupIdentifier [ ^'macvm.views' ]
    CocoaUI class >> doItIdentifier [ ^'macvm.doit' ]
    CocoaUI class >> printItIdentifier [ ^'macvm.printit' ]

    "── the item specs, in bar order ──────────────────────────────────────
     Each is (identifier, label, NSImage-or-nil, action delegate). Views and
     the font/Do It/Print It buttons all register here; the delegate turns a
     spec into a real NSToolbarItem on demand. Native items rather than
     embedded NSButtons, so AppKit draws the standard hover/press treatment,
     sizes them itself, and can put a proper menu item in the overflow
     chevron when the window gets narrow — which is what replaced the
     hand-rolled `x := 8 + (n * step)` arithmetic and the collision sweep
     that used to hide the metrics once the icons grew into them."
    CocoaUI class >> addToolbarItemIdent: ident label: aLabel icon: iconBase action: aBlock [
        ToolbarSpecs add: (self toolbarSpecIdent: ident label: aLabel icon: iconBase action: aBlock).
        ^ident
    ]

    "The template NSImage for an icons-mono/ base name. Falls back to a
     system symbol rather than nil, so a missing SVG can never put a nil into
     the segmented group's `images:` array (which would raise) — and never
     leaves an invisible control."
    CocoaUI class >> toolbarIconNamed: iconBase [
        | img |
        iconBase isNil ifFalse: [
            img := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSImage') onMain alloc) onMain
                initWithContentsOfFile: self iconDir , iconBase , '.svg' ].
        img isNil ifTrue: [
            ^(Cocoa classNamed: 'NSImage') onMain
                imageWithSystemSymbolName: 'questionmark.square'
                accessibilityDescription: nil ].
        img onMain setTemplate: true.
        ^img
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> toolbarSpecIdent: ident label: aLabel icon: iconBase action: aBlock [
        | img holder delegate |
        img := iconBase isNil ifTrue: [ nil ] ifFalse: [ self toolbarIconNamed: iconBase ].
        holder := CocoaToolbarAction on: aBlock.
        delegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: holder.
        ToolbarDelegates add: delegate.
        ^Array with: ident with: aLabel with: img with: delegate
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> toolbarSpecFor: ident [
        ToolbarSpecs isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        ^ToolbarSpecs detect: [ :spec | (spec at: 1) = ident ] ifNone: [ nil ]
    ]

    "The item order AppKit asks for: every registered item, then a flexible
     space, then the metrics. The flexible space is what pins the metrics to
     the RIGHT edge and keeps them there on resize — a toolbar's own idiom
     for it."
    CocoaUI class >> toolbarItemIdentifiers [
        | ids |
        ids := OrderedCollection new.
        ids add: self viewGroupIdentifier.
        ToolbarSpecs isNil ifFalse: [
            ToolbarSpecs do: [ :spec | ids add: (spec at: 1) ] ].
        ids add: self flexibleSpaceIdentifier.
        ids add: self metricsItemIdentifier.
        ^ids asArray
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> buildWindowToolbar [
        | tbClass |
        tbClass := Cocoa classNamed: 'NSToolbar'.
        ToolbarItems := Dictionary new.
        "Held in a class var: AppKit does NOT retain what a delegate hands
         back, so the world stays the owner of every vended object."
        ToolbarDelegate := MacvmDelegate toolbarDelegateOn: self.
        WinToolbar := (tbClass onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithIdentifier: self toolbarIdentifier.
        WinToolbar onMain setDelegate: ToolbarDelegate.
        "No customization sheet: the item set is ours, not the user's."
        WinToolbar onMain setAllowsUserCustomization: false.
        "NSToolbarDisplayModeIconOnly — no label row under the items, which
         is what a dense icon bar plus a metrics readout wants."
        WinToolbar onMain setDisplayMode: 2.
        Win onMain setToolbar: WinToolbar.
        "NSWindowToolbarStyleUnified: the title and the toolbar share one
         titlebar row — the unified look the rest of macOS uses, and what
         retires the separate 44pt strip under the titlebar."
        Win onMain setToolbarStyle: 3.
    ]

    "── NSToolbarDelegate (dispatched from Rust; each ANSWERS an object) ──"

    CocoaUI class >> toolbarAllowedItemIdentifiers: aToolbar [
        ^self toolbarIdentifierArray
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> toolbarDefaultItemIdentifiers: aToolbar [
        ^self toolbarIdentifierArray
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> toolbar: aToolbar itemForItemIdentifier: anIdentifier willBeInsertedIntoToolbar: aFlag [
        "anIdentifier arrives as an ObjcRef on an NSString; `description`
         answers the receiver for an NSString, so sendString: copies its
         characters out as a Smalltalk String we can actually compare.
         Answering nil means 'no such item', which AppKit tolerates."
        | name |
        name := anIdentifier sendString: 'description'.
        name = self metricsItemIdentifier ifTrue: [ ^self metricsToolbarItem ].
        name = self viewGroupIdentifier ifTrue: [ ^self viewGroupToolbarItem ].
        ^self buttonToolbarItemNamed: name
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> buttonToolbarItemNamed: ident [
        "Cached in ToolbarItems: AppKit asks for the same identifier more than
         once (bar, then overflow menu), and a second copy would carry a second
         action delegate."
        | item spec |
        item := ToolbarItems at: ident ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        item isNil ifFalse: [ ^item ].
        spec := self toolbarSpecFor: ident.
        spec isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        item := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSToolbarItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithItemIdentifier: ident.
        item onMain setLabel: (spec at: 2).
        item onMain setPaletteLabel: (spec at: 2).
        item onMain setToolTip: (spec at: 2).
        (spec at: 3) isNil
            ifTrue: [
                "No icon for it — show the label ON the item so it is never an
                 invisible control (the same bar registerViewNamed: set)."
                item onMain setTitle: (spec at: 2) ]
            ifFalse: [ item onMain setImage: (spec at: 3) ].
        "Big Sur+ toolbar buttons draw a rounded-square background on hover."
        item onMain setBordered: true.
        item onMain setTarget: (spec at: 4).
        item onMain setAction: 'macvmAction:'.
        ToolbarItems at: ident put: item.
        ^item
    ]

    "── the view switchers, as ONE segmented group ────────────────────────
     Nine loose icon items cost ~400pt of bar and never showed which view was
     active. An NSToolbarItemGroup in SelectOne mode draws them as a single
     segmented control — the idiom Mail/Notes/System Settings use for exactly
     this — which is both far tighter (so the metrics still fit on the right
     at a normal window width) and self-labelling: the active view's segment
     stays lit."
    CocoaUI class >> viewGroupToolbarItem [
        | item imgs labels holder delegate |
        item := ToolbarItems at: self viewGroupIdentifier ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        item isNil ifFalse: [ ^item ].
        imgs := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMutableArray') onMain alloc) onMain init.
        labels := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMutableArray') onMain alloc) onMain init.
        ViewItemSpecs do: [ :spec |
            imgs onMain addObject: (spec at: 3).
            labels onMain addObject: (Cocoa nsString: (spec at: 2)) ].
        "The CONVENIENCE constructor, not alloc/init + setSubitems:. Only the
         group it builds carries the segmented control representation that
         selectionMode/selectedIndex drive: a hand-assembled group renders as
         loose items and silently refuses setSelectedIndex: (measured — it
         stayed -1 through both a manual set and a live view switch).
         target:/action: go in afterwards through the ordinary property
         setters, because the bridge marshals a String argument as an
         NSString, not as the SEL this constructor's last parameter wants."
        "NSToolbarItemGroupSelectionModeSelectOne = 0. NOT 1 — that is
         selectAny, under which every segment you click STAYS lit and the bar
         accumulates highlights instead of moving one. (momentary = 2.)"
        item := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSToolbarItemGroup') onMain
            groupWithItemIdentifier: self viewGroupIdentifier
            images: imgs
            selectionMode: 0
            labels: labels
            target: nil
            action: nil.
        holder := CocoaToolbarAction on: [ self viewGroupClicked ].
        delegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: holder.
        ToolbarDelegates add: delegate.
        item onMain setTarget: delegate.
        item onMain setAction: 'macvmAction:'.
        item onMain setLabel: 'View'.
        item onMain setPaletteLabel: 'View'.
        ToolbarItems at: self viewGroupIdentifier put: item.
        imgs release. labels release.
        ^item
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> viewGroupClicked [
        "A segment fired. When the GROUP owns the selection there is no
         per-segment target/action — which one was hit is in selectedIndex."
        | group idx |
        group := ToolbarItems at: self viewGroupIdentifier ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        group isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        idx := group sendI64: 'selectedIndex'.
        (idx < 0 or: [ idx >= Views size ]) ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        self switchToView: ((Views at: idx + 1) at: 1).
    ]

    "Do It / Print It act on the WORKSPACE's text, so they dim on every other
     tab rather than silently doing nothing. Run from switchToView:, and once
     at startup after the first switch."
    CocoaUI class >> syncToolbarEnablement [
        | on idx |
        ToolbarItems isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        on := ActiveView == #workspace.
        (Array with: self doItIdentifier with: self printItIdentifier) do: [ :ident |
            (ToolbarItems at: ident ifAbsent: [ nil ]) ifNotNil: [ :item |
                item onMain setEnabled: on ] ].
        "Light the segment for the view we just switched to — including when
         the switch came from the View menu or a script, not the toolbar."
        (ToolbarItems at: self viewGroupIdentifier ifAbsent: [ nil ]) ifNotNil: [ :group |
            idx := ViewItemSpecs findFirst: [ :spec |
                (spec at: 1) = ('macvm.view.' , ActiveView) ].
            idx > 0 ifTrue: [ group onMain setSelectedIndex: idx - 1 ] ].
    ]

    "The identifier array, built once and KEPT — see buildWindowToolbar."
    CocoaUI class >> toolbarIdentifierArray [
        ToolbarIdentArray isNil ifTrue: [
            ToolbarIdentArray := self nsArrayOfStrings: self toolbarItemIdentifiers ].
        ^ToolbarIdentArray
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> nsArrayOfStrings: aCollection [
        "A fresh, world-owned NSArray of NSStrings. alloc/init rather than
         +array so the ObjcRef owns its +1 outright (no autorelease-pool
         lifetime to reason about across the callback boundary)."
        | arr |
        arr := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMutableArray') onMain alloc) onMain init.
        aCollection do: [ :each | arr onMain addObject: (Cocoa nsString: each) ].
        ^arr
    ]

    "── the metrics, as one custom-view toolbar item ─────────────────────"
    CocoaUI class >> metricsItemHeight [ ^30.0 ]

    CocoaUI class >> metricsToolbarItem [
        "Built once and cached: AppKit may ask for the same identifier more
         than once (toolbar, then overflow menu), and the cluster's views are
         wired into MetricLabels — a second copy would leave the first one
         frozen at its last value."
        | item |
        item := ToolbarItems at: self metricsItemIdentifier ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        item isNil ifFalse: [ ^item ].
        MetricsHost := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSView') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: 0.0 with: 0.0
                                  with: self metricsClusterWidth
                                  with: self metricsItemHeight).
        self buildMetricsClusterIn: MetricsHost.
        item := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSToolbarItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithItemIdentifier: self metricsItemIdentifier.
        item onMain setView: MetricsHost.
        item onMain setLabel: 'Metrics'.
        item onMain setPaletteLabel: 'Metrics'.
        item onMain setMinSize: (Array with: self metricsClusterWidth
                                       with: self metricsItemHeight).
        item onMain setMaxSize: (Array with: self metricsClusterWidth
                                       with: self metricsItemHeight).
        ToolbarItems at: self metricsItemIdentifier put: item.
        ^item
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> texturePatternColor [
        "The tiled grain as a pattern NSColor, or nil if the texture file is
         missing. A fresh load per strip is cheap and avoids a stale handle
         across a UI rebuild."
        | img |
        img := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSImage') onMain alloc onMain
            initWithContentsOfFile: self toolbarTexturePath.
        img isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        ^(Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain colorWithPatternImage: img
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> addTexturedBoxFrame: rect resize: mask in: aParent [
        "Fill `rect` in `aParent` with the grain pattern — a borderless custom
         NSBox, plain-color fill (no CGColorRef marshalling, same safety as
         the mem bar). Added as a subview, so whatever is added AFTER renders
         on top of it, unchanged. Fails soft: no texture file -> nothing
         added, `nil` returned. Answers the box (for teardown/z-order)."
        | color box |
        color := self texturePatternColor.
        color isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        box := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSBox') onMain alloc) onMain initWithFrame: rect.
        box onMain setBoxType: 4.   "NSBoxCustom"
        box onMain setBorderType: 0. "NSNoBorder"
        box onMain setFillColor: color.
        box onMain setAutoresizingMask: mask.
        aParent onMain addSubview: box.
        ^box
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> metricCellWidth [ ^58.0 ]
    "The cluster's bottom edge inside its host view: the mem bar sits here,
     the value row 2pt above it, the caption row 16pt above that. Parametrized
     because the cluster used to be laid into the 44-high custom strip and now
     lives in a 30-high NSToolbarItem view."
    CocoaUI class >> metricsBaseY [ ^2.0 ]

    CocoaUI class >> buildMetricsClusterIn: aToolbar [
        "Five small, TIGHTLY-packed, right-anchored two-row cells — a muted
         caption (MEM/JIT/CODE/ALLOC/GC) over its value — matching the
         WKWebView GUI's own compact toolbar metrics cluster (same VmMetrics
         fields/semantics; sampled off the PRIMARY by the Rust watchdog and
         pushed here via updateMetricsMem:jit:code:alloc:gc:memPct:). The
         whole cluster hugs the right edge (MinXMargin flexible) so it tracks
         on resize; MEM additionally carries a small used/capacity bar — the
         graphical touch the plain-text v1 was missing."
        | names w x tfClass colorClass fontClass captionFont valueFont |
        tfClass := Cocoa classNamed: 'NSTextField'.
        colorClass := Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor'.
        fontClass := Cocoa classNamed: 'NSFont'.
        captionFont := fontClass onMain systemFontOfSize: 9.0.
        valueFont := fontClass onMain monospacedDigitSystemFontOfSize: 11.0 weight: 0.0.
        names := #('GC' 'ALLOC' 'CODE' 'JIT' 'MEM').
        x := (aToolbar onMain frame) at: 3.
        MetricLabels := Dictionary new.
        "Every metric view, so a rebuild can find them all."
        MetricViews := OrderedCollection new.
        names do: [ :n |
            | caption value w |
            "MEM's value is 'used/capacity' — the widest cell by far ('540.9K/68.0M');
             the uniform width clipped it at the slash (seen in the CG7 snap)."
            w := n = 'MEM' ifTrue: [ 100.0 ] ifFalse: [ self metricCellWidth ].
            x := x - w.
            caption := (tfClass onMain alloc) onMain
                initWithFrame: (Array with: x with: self metricsBaseY + 16.0
                                      with: w - 8.0 with: 11.0).
            caption onMain setEditable: false. caption onMain setBordered: false.
            caption onMain setDrawsBackground: false. caption onMain setSelectable: false.
            caption onMain setFont: captionFont.
            caption onMain setTextColor: colorClass onMain secondaryLabelColor.
            caption onMain setStringValue: n.
            caption onMain setAutoresizingMask: self maskMinXMargin.
            aToolbar onMain addSubview: caption.
            MetricViews add: caption.

            value := (tfClass onMain alloc) onMain
                initWithFrame: (Array with: x with: self metricsBaseY + 2.0
                                      with: w - 8.0 with: 14.0).
            value onMain setEditable: false. value onMain setBordered: false.
            value onMain setDrawsBackground: false. value onMain setSelectable: false.
            value onMain setFont: valueFont.
            value onMain setStringValue: '—'.
            value onMain setAutoresizingMask: self maskMinXMargin.
            aToolbar onMain addSubview: value.
            MetricViews add: value.

            MetricLabels at: n asSymbol put: value.
            n = 'MEM' ifTrue: [ self buildMemBarAt: x width: w - 8.0 in: aToolbar ] ].
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> buildMemBarAt: x width: aWidth in: aToolbar [
        "A 2px used/capacity bar under the MEM value: a light track (the full
         cell width) and a darker fill (scaled by memPct, 0-100). NSBox, not
         a CALayer-backed NSView: `setFillColor:` takes a plain NSColor
         object — no CGColorRef return-type marshalling risk."
        | boxClass colorClass track fill |
        boxClass := Cocoa classNamed: 'NSBox'.
        colorClass := Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor'.
        track := (boxClass onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: x with: self metricsBaseY with: aWidth with: 2.0).
        track onMain setBoxType: 4. "NSBoxCustom"
        track onMain setBorderType: 0. "NSNoBorder"
        track onMain setFillColor: colorClass onMain separatorColor.
        track onMain setAutoresizingMask: self maskMinXMargin.
        aToolbar onMain addSubview: track.

        fill := (boxClass onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: x with: self metricsBaseY with: 0.0 with: 2.0).
        fill onMain setBoxType: 4.
        fill onMain setBorderType: 0.
        fill onMain setFillColor: colorClass onMain controlAccentColor.
        fill onMain setAutoresizingMask: self maskMinXMargin.
        aToolbar onMain addSubview: fill.
        MemBarFill := fill. MemBarWidth := aWidth.
        MetricViews isNil ifFalse: [ MetricViews add: track; add: fill ].
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> metricsClusterWidth [
        "MEM (100, it carries 'used/capacity') + the four uniform cells."
        ^100.0 + (4 * self metricCellWidth)
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> updateMetricsMem: memStr jit: jitStr code: codeStr alloc: allocStr gc: gcStr memPct: pct [
        "The ~4 Hz beat is also the scripting layer's deadline sweep (design
         §5): an `evaluate` whose primary never replied — it died, or the doit
         raised, which serveUiDoit: answers with no reply at all — must fail
         with a script error rather than hang the caller until AppleScript's
         own two-minute timeout. Guarded so a world without the scripting file
         is unaffected."
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaScript) ifNotNil: [ :cs | cs sweepDeadlines ].
        "Called from Rust (the primary's beat loop wakes the UI worker every
         beat now — CG5 — so this fires ~4Hz with no new NSTimer) to refresh
         the toolbar readout AND the MEM usage bar's fill width in place."
        MetricLabels isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        (MetricLabels at: #MEM ifAbsent: [ nil ]) ifNotNil: [ :tf | tf onMain setStringValue: memStr ].
        (MetricLabels at: #JIT ifAbsent: [ nil ]) ifNotNil: [ :tf | tf onMain setStringValue: jitStr ].
        (MetricLabels at: #CODE ifAbsent: [ nil ]) ifNotNil: [ :tf | tf onMain setStringValue: codeStr ].
        (MetricLabels at: #ALLOC ifAbsent: [ nil ]) ifNotNil: [ :tf | tf onMain setStringValue: allocStr ].
        (MetricLabels at: #GC ifAbsent: [ nil ]) ifNotNil: [ :tf | tf onMain setStringValue: gcStr ].
        MemBarFill isNil ifFalse: [
            | f |
            f := (pct min: 100) max: 0.
            MemBarFill onMain setFrameSize: (Array with: MemBarWidth * f / 100.0 with: 2.0) ].
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> contentHost [ ^ContentHost ]

    "── content host: a TABLESS NSTabView — the Cocoa-native view switcher.
       Each registered view is an NSTabViewItem; AppKit owns insertion,
       removal, clipping and repaint on every switch (the manual
       remove-subviews/add-subview swapper this replaces left stale drawing
       behind — views 'drew over each other'). Our themed toolbar buttons and
       the View menu ARE the tab bar; they drive selectTabViewItemAtIndex:."
    "The split view owns everything below the toolbar: content on top, the
     transcript dock underneath, with a real draggable divider between them.
     Before this the dock was a hard-coded 110px band — collapsible, but not
     resizable, which is half of what a Mac user expects from a pane like
     this. NSSplitView also persists the divider position for us via
     setAutosaveName:, so the size survives relaunch the same way the window
     frame does."
    CocoaUI class >> buildContentHostIn: content [
        | frame splitH dockH |
        frame := content onMain frame.
        "The split view owns the WHOLE content view now: the 44pt custom
         toolbar strip it used to sit under became a real NSToolbar, which
         AppKit draws in the titlebar, outside the content view entirely."
        splitH := frame at: 4.
        dockH := self transcriptCollapsed
            ifTrue: [ self transcriptStripHeight ]
            ifFalse: [ self transcriptRestoreHeight ].
        TranscriptSplit := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSSplitView') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: 0.0 with: 0.0 with: (frame at: 3) with: splitH).
        TranscriptSplit onMain setVertical: false. "horizontal divider: panes stack"
        TranscriptSplit onMain setDividerStyle: 2.  "NSSplitViewDividerStyleThin"
        TranscriptSplit onMain setAutoresizingMask:
            self maskWidthSizable + self maskHeightSizable.

        ContentHost := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSTabView') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: 0.0 with: 0.0
                                  with: (frame at: 3)
                                  with: splitH - dockH).
        ContentHost onMain setTabViewType: 6. "NSNoTabsNoBorder — chrome is ours"
        TranscriptSplit onMain addSubview: ContentHost.

        "The dock's own container — the strip and the log live in here so the
         split view has ONE subview to size, and the strip keeps riding at the
         dock's top edge however the divider moves."
        TranscriptDock := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSView') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: 0.0 with: 0.0
                                  with: (frame at: 3) with: dockH).
        TranscriptSplit onMain addSubview: TranscriptDock.
        content onMain addSubview: TranscriptSplit.
    ]

    "── the view registry ──"
    CocoaUI class >> registerViewNamed: aSymbol title: aTitle icon: iconBaseName container: containerBlock onShow: onShowBlock [
        "Add a persistent view: register its toolbar item (icon, falling back
         to the title as text if the image fails to load — never an invisible
         control) wired through its OWN CocoaToolbarAction/macvmAction: ticket.
         `containerBlock` answers the view's root NSView (building lazily on
         first show — it becomes the tab item's view, owned by the NSTabView
         from then on); `onShowBlock` runs on every switch TO the view
         (refresh requests, first responder).

         EVERY switch path goes through switchToView: — the toolbar items
         previously ran the raw activate blocks, adding views ON TOP of the
         current one (the 'views draw over each other' bug's second head; only
         the View menu did the swap)."
        Views add: (Array with: aSymbol with: aTitle with: containerBlock with: onShowBlock).
        ViewItemSpecs add: (Array
            with: 'macvm.view.' , aSymbol
            with: aTitle
            with: (self toolbarIconNamed: iconBaseName)).
    ]

    "── code font size (the A- / A+ toolbar buttons) ──────────────────────
     One global size DELTA applied on top of each code surface's OWN base
     size, so the surfaces keep their relative sizing (Workspace 13, Editor/
     Browser 12, Transcript 11) while scaling together. `fixedFontOfSize:` is
     the single chokepoint every monospace text view builds its font through
     — so a view built AFTER a size change picks the new size up for free,
     and `applyFontEverywhere` re-applies to the already-built ones. Delta
     resets to 0 on a UI-worker restart (a session preference, not persisted)."
    CocoaUI class >> fontDelta [ ^FontDelta isNil ifTrue: [ FontDelta := 0 ] ifFalse: [ FontDelta ] ]
    CocoaUI class >> fixedFontOfSize: base [
        ^(Cocoa classNamed: 'NSFont') onMain
            userFixedPitchFontOfSize: ((base + self fontDelta) max: 6)
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> increaseFont [ FontDelta := self fontDelta + 1 min: 16. self applyFontEverywhere ]
    CocoaUI class >> decreaseFont [ FontDelta := self fontDelta - 1 max: -5. self applyFontEverywhere ]
    CocoaUI class >> applyFontEverywhere [
        "Re-set the font on every built code surface. Later-loading views
         (CocoaBrowser/CocoaEditor) are asked by name — nil-safe if a package
         isn't loaded; each guards its own not-yet-built view."
        Workspace isNil ifFalse: [ Workspace onMain setFont: (self fixedFontOfSize: 13.0) ].
        Transcript isNil ifFalse: [ Transcript onMain setFont: (self fixedFontOfSize: 11.0) ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaBrowser) ifNotNil: [ :c | c applyCodeFont ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaBrowser2) ifNotNil: [ :c | c applyCodeFont ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaEditor) ifNotNil: [ :c | c applyCodeFont ].
    ]

    "── toolbar ACTION buttons (not view switchers) ───────────────────────
     A lighter sibling of registerViewNamed:: mint an icon button that runs
     an arbitrary block, without registering a tab/view. Placed left-to-right
     after the view-switcher buttons, in the same 40px step grid."
    CocoaUI class >> buildFontButtons [
        "The trailing items, after every view has registered its switcher:
         the two font-size steppers (the same icons-mono/ template SVGs the
         WKWebView toolbar ships) and Do It / Print It. The last two have no
         icon in icons-mono/, so they render as titled toolbar buttons — a
         mixed icon/text toolbar, which is ordinary Mac."
        self addToolbarItemIdent: 'macvm.font.smaller' label: 'Smaller Text'
             icon: 'smallerText' action: [ self decreaseFont ].
        self addToolbarItemIdent: 'macvm.font.bigger' label: 'Bigger Text'
             icon: 'biggerText' action: [ self increaseFont ].
        self addToolbarItemIdent: self doItIdentifier label: 'Do It'
             icon: nil action: [ self doIt ].
        self addToolbarItemIdent: self printItIdentifier label: 'Print It'
             icon: nil action: [ self printIt ].
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> switchToView: aSymbol [
        "Select the view's tab, creating its NSTabViewItem lazily on first
         visit (the container block builds the view then). AppKit swaps the
         item views in and out of the hierarchy itself — no manual subview
         surgery, no stale drawing."
        | entry idx item container |
        entry := Views detect: [ :e | (e at: 1) == aSymbol ] ifNone: [ nil ].
        entry isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        idx := TabIndexes at: aSymbol ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        idx isNil ifTrue: [
            container := (entry at: 3) value.
            container isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
            item := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSTabViewItem') onMain alloc) onMain
                initWithIdentifier: nil.
            item onMain setView: container.
            ContentHost onMain addTabViewItem: item.
            TabItems add: item.
            idx := TabItems size - 1.
            TabIndexes at: aSymbol put: idx ].
        ContentHost onMain selectTabViewItemAtIndex: idx.
        ActiveView := aSymbol.
        self syncToolbarEnablement.
        (entry at: 4) value.
    ]

    "── the Workspace view (CG6: selection-or-everything, inline Print it) ──"
    CocoaUI class >> workspaceContainer [
        "Answer the workspace's root view (a wrapper around the scroll),
         building lazily — the tab host owns it from first show onward. The
         grained Do It / Print It bar that used to ride on top is gone: both
         are toolbar items now (and ⌘D/⌘P still work), so the text gets the
         full height of the tab."
        | tvClass frame w h |
        Workspace isNil ifTrue: [
            tvClass := Cocoa classNamed: 'NSTextView'.
            frame := ContentHost onMain frame.
            w := frame at: 3. h := frame at: 4.
            WorkspaceContainer := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSView') onMain alloc) onMain
                initWithFrame: (Array with: 0.0 with: 0.0 with: w with: h).
            WorkspaceContainer onMain setAutoresizingMask: self maskWidthSizable + self maskHeightSizable.
            WorkspaceContainer onMain setAutoresizesSubviews: true.

            Workspace := (tvClass onMain alloc) onMain
                initWithFrame: (Array with: 0.0 with: 0.0 with: w with: h).
            Workspace onMain setEditable: true.
            Workspace onMain setAutomaticQuoteSubstitutionEnabled: false.
            Workspace onMain setAutomaticDashSubstitutionEnabled: false.
            Workspace onMain setAutomaticTextReplacementEnabled: false.
            Workspace onMain setFont: (self fixedFontOfSize: 13.0).
            "Scroll-wrapped like every other text surface: a bare NSTextView
             draws past its frame and its stale pixels bleed through the next
             view's transparent regions after a switch (the browser-first-open
             bug); the scroll view clips, scrolls long content, and repaints
             its area properly."
            Workspace onMain setVerticallyResizable: true.
            Workspace onMain setHorizontallyResizable: false.
            Workspace onMain setAutoresizingMask: self maskWidthSizable.
            (Workspace onMain textContainer) ifNotNil: [ :tc |
                tc onMain setWidthTracksTextView: true.
                tc release ].
            WorkspaceScroll := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSScrollView') onMain alloc) onMain
                initWithFrame: (Array with: 0.0 with: 0.0 with: w with: h).
            WorkspaceScroll onMain setHasVerticalScroller: true.
            WorkspaceScroll onMain setAutoresizingMask: self maskWidthSizable + self maskHeightSizable.
            WorkspaceScroll onMain setDocumentView: Workspace.
            WorkspaceContainer onMain addSubview: WorkspaceScroll.
            "CG8: live syntax colour, same mechanism as the browser's source
             pane (the delegate fires for programmatic setString: too)."
            WorkspaceTextDelegate := MacvmDelegate textDelegateOn: self.
            Workspace onMain setDelegate: WorkspaceTextDelegate.
            self recolorizeWorkspace.
        ].
        ^WorkspaceContainer
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> workspace [ ^Workspace ]

    "── toolbar/menu Do it / Print it (CG6: selection-aware) ──"
    CocoaUI class >> installMenu [
        "The app menu (⌘Q) is installed by Rust BEFORE startup (main.rs) so
         `mainMenu` already exists here and this ADDS the Workspace submenu
         to it, never replaces it (the CG4-review menu-clobber fix).

         CG9's UI rebuild re-runs `startup` -> `installMenu` against that
         SAME, still-live mainMenu — NSApplication's own menu bar is a
         native object that persists across a UI-worker rebuild, unlike the
         window/toolbar/content host, which teardown actually tears down.
         Left unguarded, every rebuild appended a SECOND full set of
         Workspace/View/Theme/Demos/Debug submenus on top of the first —
         visible as duplicate entries, the stale half permanently greyed
         out (their targets are delegates bound to the dead prior VM's own
         MacvmDelegate registry, which fails closed on dispatch; AppKit
         shows an item whose action can't fire as disabled).

         FIRST FIX ATTEMPT (WRONG, do not repeat): track the top-level
         items THIS method installed in a classVar, remove them next call.
         Looked right, verifiably did nothing — a rebuild boots a WHOLE
         FRESH UI-worker VM from source, so every Smalltalk-side classVar
         (including that tracking list) is nil again at the top of THIS
         very call, on every single generation, forever. Only the native
         mainMenu object itself survives a rebuild; nothing on the
         Smalltalk side does. FIX: read the fresh set's own fixed, known
         titles back OFF mainMenu directly (itemWithTitle:) and remove
         whatever's there before adding the new set — stateless from the
         VM's own perspective by construction, so it can't go stale across
         a rebuild the way a classVar can."
        | app mainMenu wsItem wsMenu printItem doItem |
        ActTarget := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: CocoaUI.
        app := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSApplication') onMain sharedApplication.
        mainMenu := app onMain mainMenu.
        mainMenu isNil ifTrue: [
            mainMenu := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenu') onMain alloc onMain init.
            app onMain setMainMenu: mainMenu ].

        #('Workspace' 'File' 'Edit' 'Source' 'View' 'Tools' 'Demos' 'Debug'
          'Window' 'Help') do: [ :title |
            | old |
            old := mainMenu onMain itemWithTitle: title.
            old isNil ifFalse: [ mainMenu onMain removeItem: old ] ].

        wsMenu := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenu') onMain alloc onMain init.
        printItem := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: 'Print It' action: 'macvmPrintIt:' keyEquivalent: 'p'.
        printItem onMain setTarget: ActTarget.
        wsMenu onMain addItem: printItem.
        doItem := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: 'Do It' action: 'macvmDoIt:' keyEquivalent: 'd'.
        doItem onMain setTarget: ActTarget.
        wsMenu onMain addItem: doItem.

        wsItem := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: 'Workspace' action: nil keyEquivalent: ''.
        wsItem onMain setSubmenu: wsMenu.
        mainMenu onMain addItem: wsItem.

        self installFileMenuOn: mainMenu.
        self installEditMenuOn: mainMenu.
        self installSourceMenuOn: mainMenu.
        self installViewMenuOn: mainMenu.
        self installToolsMenuOn: mainMenu.
        self installDemosMenuOn: mainMenu.
        self installDebugMenuOn: mainMenu.
        "Window and Help go LAST, in that order — macOS puts Help at the far
         right and expects Window immediately before it."
        self installWindowMenuOn: mainMenu.
        self installHelpMenuOn: mainMenu.
    ]

    "── File + Source menus (the editor's file workflow): every item first
       fronts the Editor view (building it lazily, exactly what the View menu
       does), then acts on it. Open…/Save As… only FLAG a host panel — the
       modal pump runs top-level in drain_perform, never in this callback. ──"
    CocoaUI class >> editorDo: aBlock [
        "Front the Editor and hand its CLASS to the one-arg block — late-bound
         by NAME (this file compiles before 68_cocoaeditor; a bareword
         CocoaEditor here is a compile error, the Debug menu's own rule)."
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaEditor) ifNotNil: [ :ec |
            self switchToView: #editor.
            aBlock value: ec ].
    ]
    "── the standard Mac menus (Edit / Window / Help) ─────────────────────"

    "Like menu:on:triples: but with NO target, which is the whole point.
     A nil target makes AppKit send the action down the RESPONDER CHAIN, so
     `copy:` reaches whichever NSTextView is focused — the workspace, an
     editor pane, the transcript — without us tracking which. Wiring these to
     a delegate instead would hard-bind one text view and break the rest, and
     AppKit also greys an item out automatically when nothing in the chain
     responds, which is the behaviour users read as 'correct'."
    CocoaUI class >> standardMenu: aTitle on: mainMenu items: someTriples [
        | menu topItem |
        menu := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenu') onMain alloc onMain init.
        menu onMain setTitle: aTitle.
        someTriples do: [ :t |
            (t at: 1) = '-'
                ifTrue: [ menu onMain addItem: (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain separatorItem ]
                ifFalse: [
                    | item |
                    item := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
                        initWithTitle: (t at: 1) action: (t at: 3) keyEquivalent: (t at: 2).
                    menu onMain addItem: item ] ].
        topItem := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: aTitle action: nil keyEquivalent: ''.
        topItem onMain setSubmenu: menu.
        mainMenu onMain addItem: topItem.
        ^menu
    ]

    "Every Mac app has this, and its absence is loud in an app whose main
     pane is a text editor: the shortcuts already worked (NSTextView answers
     them through the responder chain) but there was nowhere to LOOK, which
     is where most people go first."
    CocoaUI class >> installEditMenuOn: mainMenu [
        | items |
        items := OrderedCollection new.
        items add: (Array with: 'Undo' with: 'z' with: 'undo:').
        items add: (Array with: 'Redo' with: 'Z' with: 'redo:').
        items add: (Array with: '-' with: '' with: nil).
        items add: (Array with: 'Cut' with: 'x' with: 'cut:').
        items add: (Array with: 'Copy' with: 'c' with: 'copy:').
        items add: (Array with: 'Paste' with: 'v' with: 'paste:').
        items add: (Array with: 'Delete' with: '' with: 'delete:').
        items add: (Array with: 'Select All' with: 'a' with: 'selectAll:').
        ^self standardMenu: 'Edit' on: mainMenu items: items
    ]

    "setWindowsMenu: hands the menu to AppKit, which then keeps the list of
     open windows in it and ticks the front one — behaviour we would
     otherwise have to write and keep correct ourselves."
    CocoaUI class >> installWindowMenuOn: mainMenu [
        | items menu |
        items := OrderedCollection new.
        items add: (Array with: 'Minimize' with: 'm' with: 'performMiniaturize:').
        items add: (Array with: 'Zoom' with: '' with: 'performZoom:').
        items add: (Array with: '-' with: '' with: nil).
        items add: (Array with: 'Bring All to Front' with: '' with: 'arrangeInFront:').
        menu := self standardMenu: 'Window' on: mainMenu items: items.
        (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSApplication') onMain sharedApplication onMain
            setWindowsMenu: menu.
        ^menu
    ]

    "setHelpMenu: is what makes the Help search field at the top of the menu
     find our items; without it macOS may adopt some other menu as Help."
    CocoaUI class >> installHelpMenuOn: mainMenu [
        | menu item holder delegate topItem |
        menu := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenu') onMain alloc onMain init.
        menu onMain setTitle: 'Help'.
        item := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: 'MACVM Help' action: 'macvmAction:' keyEquivalent: '?'.
        holder := CocoaToolbarAction on: [ self switchToView: #help ].
        delegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: holder.
        item onMain setTarget: delegate.
        ToolbarDelegates add: delegate.
        menu onMain addItem: item.
        topItem := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: 'Help' action: nil keyEquivalent: ''.
        topItem onMain setSubmenu: menu.
        mainMenu onMain addItem: topItem.
        (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSApplication') onMain sharedApplication onMain
            setHelpMenu: menu.
        ^menu
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> menu: aTitle on: mainMenu triples: someTriples [
        "Build one submenu from {title. keyEquivalent. block} triples —
         the Debug menu's own delegate wiring, factored."
        | menu topItem |
        menu := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenu') onMain alloc onMain init.
        someTriples do: [ :t |
            | item holder delegate |
            item := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
                initWithTitle: (t at: 1) action: 'macvmAction:' keyEquivalent: (t at: 2).
            holder := CocoaToolbarAction on: (t at: 3).
            delegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: holder.
            item onMain setTarget: delegate.
            menu onMain addItem: item.
            ToolbarDelegates add: delegate ].
        topItem := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: aTitle action: nil keyEquivalent: ''.
        topItem onMain setSubmenu: menu.
        mainMenu onMain addItem: topItem.
        ^topItem
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> installFileMenuOn: mainMenu [
        | triples |
        triples := OrderedCollection new.
        triples add: (Array with: 'New' with: 'n' with: [ self editorDo: [ :ec | ec fileNew ] ]).
        triples add: (Array with: 'Open…' with: 'o' with: [ self editorDo: [ :ec | ec fileOpen ] ]).
        triples add: (Array with: 'Save' with: 's' with: [ self editorDo: [ :ec | ec fileSave ] ]).
        triples add: (Array with: 'Save As…' with: 'S' with: [ self editorDo: [ :ec | ec fileSaveAs ] ]).
        triples add: (Array with: 'File In' with: '' with: [ self editorDo: [ :ec | ec fileIn ] ]).
        triples add: (Array with: 'Add to World' with: '' with: [ self editorDo: [ :ec | ec addToWorld ] ]).
        self menu: 'File' on: mainMenu triples: triples.
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> installSourceMenuOn: mainMenu [
        | triples |
        triples := OrderedCollection new.
        triples add: (Array with: 'Format' with: 'F' with: [ self editorDo: [ :ec | ec formatBuffer ] ]).
        triples add: (Array with: 'Analyze' with: 'A' with: [ self editorDo: [ :ec | ec analyzeBuffer ] ]).
        self menu: 'Source' on: mainMenu triples: triples.
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> installToolsMenuOn: mainMenu [
        "Asset editors (docs/asset_editors_design.md): utilities summoned by
         menu, not view-registry tabs — they open their own windows beside
         the game window they preview into. Late-bound by name (the CG7/CG8
         discipline): the editor files load after this one in cocoaui.list."
        | menu toolsItem |
        menu := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenu') onMain alloc onMain init.
        self toolsMenu: menu item: 'Sprite Editor…' opens: #CocoaSpriteEditor.
        toolsItem := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: 'Tools' action: nil keyEquivalent: ''.
        toolsItem onMain setSubmenu: menu.
        mainMenu onMain addItem: toolsItem.
        ^toolsItem
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> toolsMenu: menu item: aTitle opens: aClassSymbol [
        | item holder delegate |
        item := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: aTitle action: 'macvmAction:' keyEquivalent: ''.
        holder := CocoaToolbarAction on: [
            (Worker classNamed: aClassSymbol) ifNotNil: [ :c | c open ] ].
        delegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: holder.
        item onMain setTarget: delegate.
        menu onMain addItem: item.
        ToolbarDelegates add: delegate.
        ^item
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> installDemosMenuOn: mainMenu [
        "CG10: launch a native GamePane demo on the PRIMARY (it spawns the
         compute workers ParallelMandel needs; its frames cross to the main
         thread's Metal pane over the worker→main protocol). Each opens the
         game in its own window; Escape ends it."
        | demosMenu demosItem pairs |
        demosMenu := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenu') onMain alloc onMain init.
        pairs := OrderedCollection new.
        pairs add: (Array with: 'Breakout' with: 'Breakout launch').
        pairs add: (Array with: 'Galaxigans' with: 'Galaxigans launch').
        pairs add: (Array with: 'MandelZoom' with: 'MandelZoom launch').
        pairs add: (Array with: 'Parallel Mandelbrot' with: 'ParallelMandel launch').
        pairs add: (Array with: 'Worms' with: 'Worms launch').
        pairs add: (Array with: 'FFT Scope' with: 'FftScope launch').
        pairs do: [ :pair |
            | title entry item holder delegate |
            title := pair at: 1. entry := pair at: 2.
            item := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
                initWithTitle: title action: 'macvmAction:' keyEquivalent: ''.
            "Launch via the host service (TOP-LEVEL on the primary), NOT a
             nested uiDoit — the frame loop's GC roots can't survive nesting."
            holder := CocoaToolbarAction on: [
                ((Cocoa classNamed: 'MacvmHostService') onMain alloc) onMain init
                    onMain launchDemo: entry ].
            delegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: holder.
            item onMain setTarget: delegate.
            demosMenu onMain addItem: item.
            ToolbarDelegates add: delegate ].
        demosItem := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: 'Demos' action: nil keyEquivalent: ''.
        demosItem onMain setSubmenu: demosMenu.
        mainMenu onMain addItem: demosItem.
        ^demosItem
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> installDebugMenuOn: mainMenu [
        "CG9: a Debug menu to exercise the recovery machinery on-screen —
         'Rebuild UI' (Layer 2: tears down + rebuilds the whole window set on
         a fresh UI worker VM; the primary and its state are untouched),
         'Restart Primary VM' (the SAME respawn-from-source a fatal doit
         triggers automatically, on demand — the primary's whole object
         heap is discarded and rebooted from the world source; the UI shell
         is untouched and re-syncs onto the fresh primary automatically),
         and 'Force Fault' (Layer 1: a deliberate bad Alien deref in a
         callback, recovered by the C6 door to its shape default — the run
         loop pumps on)."
        | debugMenu debugItem pairs jitHolder jitDelegate |
        debugMenu := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenu') onMain alloc onMain init.
        pairs := OrderedCollection new.
        pairs add: (Array with: 'Break on Entry (Browser V2 selection)' with: [ self breakOnBrowserSelection: true ]).
        pairs add: (Array with: 'Clear Break (Browser V2 selection)' with: [ self breakOnBrowserSelection: false ]).
        pairs add: (Array with: 'Rebuild UI' with: [ self requestRebuild ]).
        pairs add: (Array with: 'Restart Primary VM' with: [ self requestPrimaryRestart ]).
        pairs add: (Array with: 'Force Fault (recovered)' with: [ self forceFault ]).
        pairs do: [ :pair |
            | title item holder delegate |
            title := pair at: 1.
            item := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
                initWithTitle: title action: 'macvmAction:' keyEquivalent: ''.
            holder := CocoaToolbarAction on: (pair at: 2).
            delegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: holder.
            item onMain setTarget: delegate.
            debugMenu onMain addItem: item.
            ToolbarDelegates add: delegate ].
        "Compiler (JIT) — a checkable toggle (default ON). OFF stops the VM
         compiling NEW methods, so anything not already compiled runs
         interpreted; ON resumes normal tier-up. Global (all VMs); a plain
         host-atomic flip, no VM re-entry."
        JitOn := true.
        JitMenuItem := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: 'Compiler (JIT)' action: 'macvmAction:' keyEquivalent: ''.
        JitMenuItem onMain setState: 1.
        jitHolder := CocoaToolbarAction on: [ self toggleJit ].
        jitDelegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: jitHolder.
        JitMenuItem onMain setTarget: jitDelegate.
        debugMenu onMain addItem: JitMenuItem.
        ToolbarDelegates add: jitDelegate.

        "Halt on Error (DBG4, default ON): a would-be-fatal error/DNU on the
         primary opens the Debugger tab at the fault instead of printing a
         trace and recovering. A host-atomic flip, synced onto the primary."
        HaltOn := true.
        HaltMenuItem := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: 'Halt on Error' action: 'macvmAction:' keyEquivalent: ''.
        HaltMenuItem onMain setState: 1.
        jitHolder := CocoaToolbarAction on: [ self toggleHaltOnError ].
        jitDelegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: jitHolder.
        HaltMenuItem onMain setTarget: jitDelegate.
        debugMenu onMain addItem: HaltMenuItem.
        ToolbarDelegates add: jitDelegate.

        debugItem := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: 'Debug' action: nil keyEquivalent: ''.
        debugItem onMain setSubmenu: debugMenu.
        mainMenu onMain addItem: debugItem.
        ^debugItem
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> toggleJit [
        "Flip the global JIT-compile switch and reflect it in the menu checkmark."
        JitOn := JitOn not.
        ((Cocoa classNamed: 'MacvmHostService') onMain alloc) onMain init
            onMain setJitCompile: (JitOn ifTrue: [ '1' ] ifFalse: [ '0' ]).
        JitMenuItem isNil ifFalse: [
            JitMenuItem onMain setState: (JitOn ifTrue: [ 1 ] ifFalse: [ 0 ]) ].
        self appendTranscript: 'compiler (JIT) ', (JitOn ifTrue: [ 'ON' ] ifFalse: [ 'OFF (interpreting new methods)' ]).
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> breakOnBrowserSelection: setIt [
        "DBG4 Break on Entry: plant (or clear) a breakpoint on Browser V2's
         currently-selected method. The host parks it; the primary pump applies
         it on its own thread and reports on the Transcript. A halt then opens
         the Debugger tab the next time that method is entered."
        | b2 cls sel svc |
        b2 := Worker classNamed: #CocoaBrowser2.
        b2 isNil ifTrue: [ ^self appendTranscript: 'debugger: Browser V2 not loaded' ].
        cls := b2 selectedBreakpointClass.
        sel := b2 selectedMethodName.
        (cls isNil or: [ sel isNil ]) ifTrue: [
            ^self appendTranscript: 'debugger: select a method in Browser V2 first' ].
        "Build the host service directly (this file loads before CocoaBrowser,
         so `CocoaBrowser hostService` would be a compile error here)."
        svc := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'MacvmHostService') onMain alloc) onMain init.
        setIt
            ifTrue: [ svc onMain setBreakpointClass: cls selector: sel ]
            ifFalse: [ svc onMain clearBreakpointClass: cls selector: sel ].
        self appendTranscript: 'debugger: ', (setIt ifTrue: [ 'break on ' ] ifFalse: [ 'clear break on ' ]), cls, '>>', sel.
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> toggleHaltOnError [
        "Flip the DBG4 halt-on-error switch (host atomic; the primary's pump
         reads it each beat) and reflect it in the menu checkmark."
        HaltOn := HaltOn not.
        ((Cocoa classNamed: 'MacvmHostService') onMain alloc) onMain init
            onMain setHaltOnError: (HaltOn ifTrue: [ '1' ] ifFalse: [ '0' ]).
        HaltMenuItem isNil ifFalse: [
            HaltMenuItem onMain setState: (HaltOn ifTrue: [ 1 ] ifFalse: [ 0 ]) ].
        self appendTranscript: 'halt on error ', (HaltOn ifTrue: [ 'ON' ] ifFalse: [ 'OFF (errors print a trace + recover)' ]).
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> requestRebuild [
        "Ask the host to rebuild the UI worker in place (serviced on the next
         main-thread drain, never inside this callback)."
        ((Cocoa classNamed: 'MacvmHostService') onMain alloc) onMain init
            onMain requestUiRebuild.
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> requestPrimaryRestart [
        "Ask the host to respawn the primary VM from source (serviced on the
         next main-thread drain, never inside this callback) — the exact
         same watchdog path a fatal doit triggers automatically, fired on
         demand for testing/debugging."
        ((Cocoa classNamed: 'MacvmHostService') onMain alloc) onMain init
            onMain requestPrimaryRestart.
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> forceFault [
        "A deliberate native fault inside a C6 callback — the door recovers it
         to the shape default (Layer 1). Requesting a rebuild first proves a
         recovered fault can escalate to a full rebuild (Layer 2)."
        self requestRebuild.
        ^(Alien forAddress: 8 size: 8) byteAt: 1
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> installViewMenuOn: mainMenu [
        "Built FROM the view registry (§ registerViewNamed:...), never hand-
         maintained — one item per persistent view, sharing the exact
         macvmAction: mechanism the toolbar's own buttons use (CG5)."
        | viewMenu viewItem tHolder tDelegate |
        viewMenu := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenu') onMain alloc onMain init.
        Views do: [ :e |
            | sym title item holder delegate |
            sym := e at: 1. title := e at: 2.
            item := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
                initWithTitle: title action: 'macvmAction:' keyEquivalent: ''.
            holder := CocoaToolbarAction on: [ self switchToView: sym ].
            delegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: holder.
            item onMain setTarget: delegate.
            viewMenu onMain addItem: item.
            ToolbarDelegates add: delegate ].
        "Separator, then the dock toggle — the menu route to the same state
         the strip's chevron drives. Cmd-Shift-T, next to the other view
         switches."
        viewMenu onMain addItem: (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain separatorItem.
        tHolder := CocoaToolbarAction on: [ self toggleTranscript ].
        tDelegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: tHolder.
        TranscriptMenuItem := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: self transcriptMenuTitle action: 'macvmAction:' keyEquivalent: 'T'.
        TranscriptMenuItem onMain setTarget: tDelegate.
        viewMenu onMain addItem: TranscriptMenuItem.
        "Retain the DELEGATE (not the holder) — same as the loop above; it is
         the object AppKit sends macvmAction: to."
        ToolbarDelegates add: tDelegate.

        "Separator, then Appearance — where macOS puts it, rather than the
         root-level `Theme` menu this used to be."
        viewMenu onMain addItem: (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain separatorItem.
        self installAppearanceMenuOn: viewMenu.

        viewItem := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: 'View' action: nil keyEquivalent: ''.
        viewItem onMain setSubmenu: viewMenu.
        mainMenu onMain addItem: viewItem.
        ^viewItem
    ]

    "── appearance: Light / Dark / follow the system ──────────────────────
     A submenu of View rather than a top-level `Theme` menu: no Mac app has
     a root-level appearance menu, and the whole point of this arc was to
     stop looking like one that does.

     The toolbar's icons are NSImage TEMPLATE images (isTemplate: true, see
     toolbarIconNamed:), which AppKit re-tints from the effective appearance
     — so the icon set needs no dark variant. Everything else in the window
     paints with SEMANTIC colours (labelColor, secondaryLabelColor,
     separatorColor, controlAccentColor, and the colorizer's systemBlue/
     Brown/Purple/Red), which are dynamic and re-resolve on their own; and
     the grain texture is an ALPHA noise PNG, so it darkens whatever is
     behind it instead of painting a fixed slab. That is why this works with
     no palette of our own — full 13-theme parity with the WKWebView GUI is
     still deferred (project_gui_themes)."
    CocoaUI class >> installAppearanceMenuOn: viewMenu [
        | menu item pairs |
        menu := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenu') onMain alloc onMain init.
        ThemeMenuItems := Dictionary new.
        pairs := OrderedCollection new.
        pairs add: (Array with: 'System' with: #system).
        pairs add: (Array with: 'Light' with: #light).
        pairs add: (Array with: 'Dark' with: #dark).
        pairs do: [ :pair |
            | title sym mi holder delegate |
            title := pair at: 1. sym := pair at: 2.
            mi := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
                initWithTitle: title action: 'macvmAction:' keyEquivalent: ''.
            holder := CocoaToolbarAction on: [ self applyTheme: sym ].
            delegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: holder.
            mi onMain setTarget: delegate.
            menu onMain addItem: mi.
            ThemeMenuItems at: sym put: mi.
            ToolbarDelegates add: delegate ].
        item := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMenuItem') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithTitle: 'Appearance' action: nil keyEquivalent: ''.
        item onMain setSubmenu: menu.
        viewMenu onMain addItem: item.
        ^item
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> themeDefaultsKey [ ^'MACVMAppearance' ]
    CocoaUI class >> themeOrder [ ^Array with: #system with: #light with: #dark ]
    CocoaUI class >> theme [ ^Theme isNil ifTrue: [ #system ] ifFalse: [ Theme ] ]

    "Remembered across launches, like the window frame and the transcript
     dock — an app that forgets which appearance you picked is not following
     the platform. Stored as an INDEX, not a name: NSUserDefaults'
     stringForKey: answers an opaque NSString the bridge cannot compare to a
     Smalltalk String, so a string preference would silently never restore."
    CocoaUI class >> loadTheme [
        | i |
        i := self userDefaults onMain integerForKey: self themeDefaultsKey.
        (i isNil or: [ i < 1 or: [ i > self themeOrder size ] ]) ifTrue: [ i := 1 ].
        Theme := self themeOrder at: i.
        ^Theme
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> saveTheme [
        | i |
        i := self themeOrder indexOf: self theme.
        i = 0 ifTrue: [ i := 1 ].
        self userDefaults onMain setInteger: i forKey: self themeDefaultsKey.
        ^self
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> applyTheme: aSymbol [
        "Scoped to NSApp, not to the window. An appearance is an APPLICATION
         preference: panels, the About box and any secondary window have to
         follow it too — a forced-dark window next to a system-light open
         panel is the giveaway that the setting was only ever skin deep.
         #system means 'no override', which is how AppKit spells 'follow the
         OS' (and is what makes a live System Settings flip take effect)."
        | name appearance |
        Theme := aSymbol.
        self saveTheme.
        self syncAppearanceMenu.
        aSymbol == #light ifTrue: [ name := 'NSAppearanceNameAqua' ].
        aSymbol == #dark ifTrue: [ name := 'NSAppearanceNameDarkAqua' ].
        appearance := name isNil
            ifTrue: [ nil ]
            ifFalse: [ (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSAppearance') onMain
                appearanceNamed: (Cocoa nsString: name) ].
        (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSApplication') onMain sharedApplication onMain
            setAppearance: appearance.
        "The window used to carry the override itself; clear it so NSApp's
         choice is what actually wins."
        Win isNil ifFalse: [ Win onMain setAppearance: nil ].
        ^Theme
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> syncAppearanceMenu [
        "The checkmark. Three mutually-exclusive choices with no indication of
         which is live is the other half of not looking like a Mac app."
        ThemeMenuItems isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        ThemeMenuItems keysAndValuesDo: [ :sym :item |
            "NSControlStateValueOn = 1, NSControlStateValueOff = 0."
            item onMain setState: (sym == self theme ifTrue: [ 1 ] ifFalse: [ 0 ]) ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── C6 action-target callbacks (dispatched synchronously on main) ──"
    CocoaUI class >> macvmPrintIt: sender [ ^self printIt ]
    CocoaUI class >> macvmDoIt: sender [ ^self doIt ]
    CocoaUI class >> textDidChange: aNote [ self recolorizeWorkspace ]
    CocoaUI class >> recolorizeWorkspace [
        "The Workspace's recolour (CG8) — the colorizer is host-side,
         reached via the browser's cached service handle (late-bound: 66
         loads after this file). Called by the text delegate on keystrokes
         AND explicitly after programmatic setString: (which posts no
         textDidChange:)."
        | ts bc |
        Workspace isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        bc := Worker classNamed: #CocoaBrowser.
        bc isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        ts := Workspace onMain textStorage.
        ts isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        "The CHECKED variant: sends the image doesn't know paint red (the
         editor and browser panes keep plain colorizeStorage: — they write
         classes whose selectors don't exist yet)."
        bc hostService onMain colorizeWorkspaceStorage: ts.
        ts release.
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> evalTargetFor: full loc: loc len: len [
        "PURE (headless-testable — the CG6 gate): the selection-or-everything
         rule (WKWebView GUI's workspaceEvalTarget, ported verbatim): a
         non-empty selection wins; an empty (collapsed) selection falls back
         to the whole buffer. loc/len are the NSRange's 0-based location and
         length. Answers an Array #(source insertAt)."
        len = 0 ifTrue: [ ^Array with: full with: full size ].
        ^Array with: (full copyFrom: loc + 1 to: loc + len) with: loc + len
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> selectionOrAll [
        "Read the live NSTextView's selection and apply the pure rule above."
        | range |
        range := Workspace onMain selectedRange.
        ^self evalTargetFor: self workspaceText loc: (range at: 1) len: (range at: 2)
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> doIt [
        | target source |
        target := self selectionOrAll.
        source := target at: 1.
        source isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        Worker uiDoit: source onReplyTimed: [ :result :ms |
            CocoaUI appendTranscript: source , ' => ' , result , ' (' , ms printString , ' ms)' ].
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> printIt [
        "Capture the insertion point NOW (at invocation) — the selection can
         move before the async #uiReply lands (the web GUI's
         pendingPrintInsertAt discipline, ported verbatim)."
        | target source insertAt |
        target := self selectionOrAll.
        source := target at: 1.
        insertAt := target at: 2.
        source isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        Worker uiDoit: source onReplyTimed: [ :result :ms |
            CocoaUI insertPrintResult: result at: insertAt.
            CocoaUI appendTranscript: source , ' => ' , result , ' (' , ms printString , ' ms)' ].
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> splice: result into: full at: anInteger [
        "PURE (headless-testable — the CG6 gate): splice ' result' into full
         right after position anInteger, clamped into [0, full size] (the
         captured insertion point can exceed the current text when the buffer
         shrank between Print It and the async reply). Answers
         #(newText caretAfter)."
        | at text |
        at := (anInteger min: full size) max: 0.
        text := ' ' , result.
        ^Array with: (full copyFrom: 1 to: at) , text , (full copyFrom: at + 1 to: full size)
               with: at + text size
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> insertPrintResult: result at: anInteger [
        | spliced |
        Workspace isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        spliced := self splice: result into: self workspaceText at: anInteger.
        Workspace onMain setString: (spliced at: 1).
        Workspace onMain setSelectedRange: (Array with: (spliced at: 2) with: 0).
        self recolorizeWorkspace.
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> workspaceText [
        "The Workspace NSTextView's current text as a Smalltalk String. `string`
         returns an NSString, which `sendString:` copies to a Smalltalk String —
         NOT `UTF8String` (that returns a `char*`, which `sendString:` would
         misread as an object id and crash — the CG4 SIGBUS fix)."
        Workspace isNil ifTrue: [ ^'' ].
        ^Workspace sendString: 'string'
    ]

    "── the transcript: a SMALL, reverse-chronological dock at the bottom ──"
    CocoaUI class >> transcriptStripHeight [ ^22.0 ]

    "── accessors the scripting surface reads (74_cocoascript.mst) ────────
     Plain readers over state this class already owns, named so the Apple
     Event layer never reaches into a class var directly."

    "The pane on screen, as its registry symbol (#workspace, #canvas, …)."
    CocoaUI class >> activeView [ ^ActiveView ]

    "The whole transcript log, newest line first — the buffer appendTranscript:
     maintains, not a re-read of the NSTextView."
    CocoaUI class >> transcriptText [
        ^TranscriptText isNil ifTrue: [ '' ] ifFalse: [ TranscriptText ]
    ]

    "Replace the Workspace buffer and re-colourise, the same pair
     insertPrintResult:at: performs after a programmatic setString: (which
     posts no textDidChange:, so the recolourise must be explicit)."
    CocoaUI class >> setWorkspaceText: aString [
        Workspace isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        Workspace onMain setString: aString.
        self recolorizeWorkspace
    ]

    "── the transcript dock's collapse state ──────────────────────────────"

    CocoaUI class >> transcriptCollapsed [ ^TranscriptCollapsed = true ]

    "The dock's total height right now: the full pane when open, just the
     control strip when shut."
    "The dock's ACTUAL height right now. Since the divider became draggable
     this is whatever the user left it at — not a constant — so it is read
     from the view rather than computed. Answers 0.0 before the dock exists."
    CocoaUI class >> transcriptDockHeight [
        TranscriptDock isNil ifTrue: [ ^0.0 ].
        ^(TranscriptDock onMain frame) at: 4
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> transcriptSplitView [ ^TranscriptSplit ]

    CocoaUI class >> transcriptToggleTitle [
        "Points the way the dock will go: down = it is open and will close."
        ^self transcriptCollapsed ifTrue: [ '▸' ] ifFalse: [ '▾' ]
    ]

    "The dock's state is a preference, not session state — a user who shuts
     the log expects it shut next launch. Uses setBool:/boolForKey:, NOT
     setObject: with a string: the bridge marshals BOOL returns to real
     Smalltalk booleans, whereas an NSString comes back as an opaque ObjcRef
     that would compare unequal to every Smalltalk string — so a string-based
     round trip silently never restores. (An unset key reads false, which is
     the right default: the dock starts open.)"
    CocoaUI class >> transcriptDefaultsKey [ ^'MACVMTranscriptCollapsed' ]

    CocoaUI class >> userDefaults [
        ^(Cocoa classNamed: 'NSUserDefaults') onMain standardUserDefaults
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> loadTranscriptCollapsed [
        TranscriptCollapsed :=
            (self userDefaults onMain boolForKey: self transcriptDefaultsKey) = true.
        ^TranscriptCollapsed
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> saveTranscriptCollapsed [
        self userDefaults onMain
            setBool: self transcriptCollapsed forKey: self transcriptDefaultsKey.
        ^self
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> toggleTranscript [
        "Capture the height BEFORE collapsing, so re-opening returns to
         whatever the user had dragged rather than the built-in default."
        self transcriptCollapsed ifFalse: [ self saveTranscriptHeight ].
        TranscriptCollapsed := self transcriptCollapsed not.
        self saveTranscriptCollapsed.
        self layoutTranscriptDock.
        TranscriptMenuItem isNil ifFalse: [
            TranscriptMenuItem onMain setTitle: self transcriptMenuTitle ].
        ^TranscriptCollapsed
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> transcriptMenuTitle [
        ^self transcriptCollapsed ifTrue: [ 'Show Transcript' ] ifFalse: [ 'Hide Transcript' ]
    ]

    "Drive the split view's divider. The dock's own pieces no longer move:
     the strip is pinned to the dock's top edge and the log fills the rest, so
     setting ONE number — the divider position — resizes everything. Collapsed
     means the dock is exactly its control strip; expanded restores the last
     dragged height (or the default if it has never been dragged)."
    "Move the divider so the dock ends up `h` points tall. Position is
     measured from the split view's TOP edge down to the divider, so the
     dock's height is whatever is left underneath it."
    CocoaUI class >> setTranscriptDockHeight: h [
        | splitH |
        TranscriptSplit isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        splitH := (TranscriptSplit onMain frame) at: 4.
        TranscriptSplit onMain
            setPosition: splitH - h - (TranscriptSplit onMain dividerThickness)
            ofDividerAtIndex: 0.
        ^self
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> layoutTranscriptDock [
        TranscriptSplit isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        self setTranscriptDockHeight: (self transcriptCollapsed
            ifTrue: [ self transcriptStripHeight ]
            ifFalse: [ self transcriptRestoreHeight ]).
        TranscriptClearBtn isNil ifFalse: [
            "Nothing to clear while it is shut, and hiding it makes the strip
             read as a title bar rather than a truncated pane."
            TranscriptClearBtn onMain setHidden: self transcriptCollapsed ].
        TranscriptToggleBtn isNil ifFalse: [
            TranscriptToggleBtn onMain setTitle: self transcriptToggleTitle ].
        ^self
    ]

    "The height to come back to when re-opened: whatever the divider was last
     dragged to, remembered across launches, defaulting to the designed 110.
     Clamped so a user who dragged the dock almost shut does not reopen it to
     a sliver that looks broken."
    CocoaUI class >> transcriptRestoreHeight [
        | saved |
        saved := self userDefaults onMain
            doubleForKey: 'MACVMTranscriptHeight'.
        (saved isNil or: [ saved < (self transcriptStripHeight * 2) ])
            ifTrue: [ ^self transcriptHeight ].
        ^saved
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> saveTranscriptHeight [
        | h |
        (TranscriptDock isNil or: [ self transcriptCollapsed ]) ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        h := (TranscriptDock onMain frame) at: 4.
        h > self transcriptStripHeight ifTrue: [
            self userDefaults onMain setDouble: h forKey: 'MACVMTranscriptHeight' ].
        ^self
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> buildTranscriptIn: ignored [
        | tvClass frame w scrollH colorClass boxClass content dockH |
        "Ignores the window's content view: the dock is a pane of the split
         view now, and everything below is placed inside THAT."
        content := TranscriptDock.
        tvClass := Cocoa classNamed: 'NSTextView'.
        colorClass := Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor'.
        boxClass := Cocoa classNamed: 'NSBox'.
        frame := content onMain frame.
        w := frame at: 3.
        dockH := frame at: 4.

        "The grain strip behind the Clear button — same texture as the
         toolbar, so the transcript dock's control bar isn't a blank sheet
         either. Added FIRST so the divider + Clear button render on top."
        "Positions come from the DOCK'S OWN height, not the transcriptHeight
         constant: the dock is built at whatever height was remembered, so a
         constant here left the strip floating mid-pane with a gap above it."
        TranscriptGrain := self addTexturedBoxFrame:
            (Array with: 0.0 with: dockH - self transcriptStripHeight
                   with: w with: self transcriptStripHeight)
            resize: self maskWidthSizable + self maskMinYMargin
            in: content.

        "No hand-drawn divider any more: NSSplitView draws (and drags) its
         own between the panes, which is both the standard look and the thing
         the user grabs. Two stacked 1px lines read as a rendering bug."

        "A thin strip for the Clear button, just below the divider — same
         'controls above content' shape CocoaCanvas/CocoaFind already use."
        TranscriptClearBtn := self stripButton: 'Clear'
            frame: (Array with: w - 54.0 with: dockH - self transcriptStripHeight
                          with: 48.0 with: 18.0)
            action: [ self clearTranscript ]
            in: content.
        TranscriptClearBtn onMain setAutoresizingMask: self maskMinXMargin + self maskMinYMargin.

        "The collapse control, at the LEFT of the same strip. A chevron that
         points down when the dock is open and right when it is shut — the
         disclosure idiom, in the flat style the rest of this strip uses. The
         strip itself never hides, so there is always something to click to
         get the log back (a dock that vanishes entirely is a support call)."
        TranscriptToggleBtn := self stripButton: self transcriptToggleTitle
            frame: (Array with: 6.0 with: dockH - self transcriptStripHeight
                          with: 24.0 with: 18.0)
            action: [ self toggleTranscript ]
            in: content.
        TranscriptToggleBtn onMain setAutoresizingMask: self maskMaxXMargin + self maskMinYMargin.

        "Scroll-wrapped: a BARE NSTextView draws past its frame when the text
         outgrows it — long transcripts bled UP over the content view (the
         CG7 'mess' screenshots). The scroll view clips and scrolls."
        scrollH := dockH - self transcriptStripHeight.
        TranscriptScroll := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSScrollView') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: 0.0 with: 0.0 with: w with: scrollH).
        TranscriptScroll onMain setHasVerticalScroller: true.
        "NSLineBorder — a plain scroll-view edge reads as barely-there; this
         draws a clean 1px box around the whole log (user report: 'a more
         distinct boundary... its not very visible yet')."
        TranscriptScroll onMain setBorderType: 1.
        TranscriptScroll onMain setAutoresizingMask:
            self maskWidthSizable + self maskHeightSizable.
        Transcript := (tvClass onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: 0.0 with: 0.0 with: w with: scrollH).
        Transcript onMain setEditable: false.
        Transcript onMain setFont: (self fixedFontOfSize: 11.0).
        Transcript onMain setVerticallyResizable: true.
        Transcript onMain setHorizontallyResizable: false.
        Transcript onMain setAutoresizingMask: self maskWidthSizable.
        TranscriptScroll onMain setDocumentView: Transcript.
        content onMain addSubview: TranscriptScroll.
    ]

    "Mint a small flat button wired through the SAME CocoaToolbarAction/
     macvmAction: ticket path registerViewNamed:... uses — CocoaCanvas/
     CocoaFind's own stripButton:frame:action: shape, added onto aParent
     directly (they add onto their own Container class var; CocoaUI has
     none, so the parent is explicit) and tracked in ToolbarDelegates so
     teardown releases it with everything else."
    CocoaUI class >> stripButton: title frame: rect action: aBlock in: aParent [
        | btn holder delegate |
        btn := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSButton') onMain alloc) onMain initWithFrame: rect.
        btn onMain setTitle: title.
        btn onMain setBezelStyle: 1.
        btn onMain setFont: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSFont') onMain systemFontOfSize: 10.0).
        holder := CocoaToolbarAction on: aBlock.
        delegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: holder.
        btn onMain setTarget: delegate.
        btn onMain setAction: 'macvmAction:'.
        ToolbarDelegates isNil ifTrue: [ ToolbarDelegates := OrderedCollection new ].
        ToolbarDelegates add: delegate.
        aParent onMain addSubview: btn.
        ^btn
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> appendTranscript: aString [
        "PREPEND — newest line first (reverse chronological, CG5): a log to
         glance at, not read top to bottom. Blast the WHOLE text each change
         (feedback_blast_dont_patch) — never an incremental NSTextView patch.
         Always snaps the visible scroll position back to the very top
         afterward: newest-first ordering alone only holds until the log
         outgrows the dock and someone (a prior manual scroll, or growth
         itself) leaves the viewport anywhere else — a 'log to glance at'
         must always show the latest line without the viewer scrolling for it."
        TranscriptText isNil ifTrue: [ TranscriptText := '' ].
        TranscriptText := aString , String lf , TranscriptText.
        Transcript isNil ifFalse: [
            Transcript onMain setString: TranscriptText.
            Transcript onMain scrollRangeToVisible: (Array with: 0 with: 0) ].
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> clearTranscript [
        TranscriptText := ''.
        Transcript isNil ifFalse: [ Transcript onMain setString: '' ].
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> environmentRestarted [
        "The watchdog respawned the primary from source and re-synced us (§5.1).
         Recover CLEAN (CG4 review): abandon every continuation still waiting on
         the DEAD primary — those replies can never come — so nothing lingers or
         leaks across restarts; then note it. The Workspace text is untouched (it
         is local)."
        Worker abandonPending.
        self appendTranscript: '— environment restarted (primary respawned) —'.
        "Snapshot views re-sync against the FRESH primary (CG7)."
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaBrowser) ifNotNil: [ :bc | bc environmentRestarted ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaBrowser2) ifNotNil: [ :b2 | b2 environmentRestarted ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaEditor) ifNotNil: [ :ec | ec environmentRestarted ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaOutliner) ifNotNil: [ :oc | oc environmentRestarted ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaCanvas) ifNotNil: [ :cv | cv environmentRestarted ].
        (Worker classNamed: #CocoaDebugger) ifNotNil: [ :dc | dc environmentRestarted ].
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> teardown [
        "Release the previous window's wrappers (idempotent — a rebuilt or
         restarted UI leaks nothing)."
        Win isNil ifFalse: [
            Win onMain orderOut: nil.
            (Worker classNamed: #CocoaBrowser) ifNotNil: [ :bc | bc teardown ].
            (Worker classNamed: #CocoaBrowser2) ifNotNil: [ :b2 | b2 teardown ].
            (Worker classNamed: #CocoaFind) ifNotNil: [ :fc | fc teardown ].
            (Worker classNamed: #CocoaEditor) ifNotNil: [ :ec | ec teardown ].
            (Worker classNamed: #CocoaOutliner) ifNotNil: [ :oc | oc teardown ].
            (Worker classNamed: #CocoaCanvas) ifNotNil: [ :cv | cv teardown ].
            (Worker classNamed: #CocoaSpriteEditor) ifNotNil: [ :se | se teardown ].
            (Worker classNamed: #CocoaHelp) ifNotNil: [ :hc | hc teardown ].
            (Worker classNamed: #CocoaDebugger) ifNotNil: [ :dc | dc teardown ].
            ActTarget isNil ifFalse: [ ActTarget isValid ifTrue: [ ActTarget release ] ].
            ToolbarDelegates isNil ifFalse: [
                ToolbarDelegates do: [ :d | d isValid ifTrue: [ d release ] ] ].
            Workspace isNil ifFalse: [ Workspace isValid ifTrue: [ Workspace release ] ].
            WorkspaceScroll isNil ifFalse: [ WorkspaceScroll isValid ifTrue: [ WorkspaceScroll release ] ].
            WorkspaceScroll := nil.
            WorkspaceContainer isNil ifFalse: [ WorkspaceContainer isValid ifTrue: [ WorkspaceContainer release ] ].
            WorkspaceContainer := nil.
            WorkspaceTextDelegate isNil ifFalse: [ WorkspaceTextDelegate isValid ifTrue: [ WorkspaceTextDelegate release ] ].
            WorkspaceTextDelegate := nil.
            ContentHost isNil ifFalse: [ ContentHost isValid ifTrue: [ ContentHost release ] ].
            Transcript isNil ifFalse: [ Transcript isValid ifTrue: [ Transcript release ] ].
            TranscriptScroll isNil ifFalse: [ TranscriptScroll isValid ifTrue: [ TranscriptScroll release ] ].
            TranscriptClearBtn isNil ifFalse: [ TranscriptClearBtn isValid ifTrue: [ TranscriptClearBtn release ] ].
            TabItems isNil ifFalse: [
                TabItems do: [ :t | t isValid ifTrue: [ t release ] ] ].
            TabItems := nil. TabIndexes := nil.
            "The NSToolbar and everything the delegate vended. Detach it from
             the window FIRST so AppKit stops asking the delegate for items
             while we are releasing them."
            Win isValid ifTrue: [ Win onMain setToolbar: nil ].
            ToolbarItems isNil ifFalse: [
                ToolbarItems do: [ :i | i isValid ifTrue: [ i release ] ] ].
            ToolbarIdentArray isNil ifFalse: [
                ToolbarIdentArray isValid ifTrue: [ ToolbarIdentArray release ] ].
            WinToolbar isNil ifFalse: [
                WinToolbar isValid ifTrue: [ WinToolbar release ] ].
            WinToolbar := nil. ToolbarDelegate := nil. ToolbarItems := nil.
            MetricsHost := nil. ToolbarIdentArray := nil.
            Win isValid ifTrue: [ Win release ].
            Win := nil. Workspace := nil. ContentHost := nil.
            Transcript := nil. TranscriptScroll := nil. ActTarget := nil. ToolbarDelegates := nil.
            TranscriptClearBtn := nil.
            ToolbarSpecs := nil. ViewItemSpecs := nil. ThemeMenuItems := nil.
            MetricLabels := nil. Views := nil.
            MemBarFill := nil. MemBarWidth := nil ]
    ]

    "── scripting: write a PNG of the window, TITLEBAR INCLUDED ───────────
     Rendered in-process off the window's theme frame (contentView's
     superview — contentView alone omits the titlebar, and with it the
     NSToolbar). cacheDisplayInRect: draws into our own bitmap, so unlike
     `screencapture -l` this needs no screen-recording permission and works
     on a window that is not frontmost. Answers self; check the file.

     KNOWN LIMITATION — do not chase this again. cacheDisplayInRect: draws
     outside any window's context, and whenever NSApp carries an EXPLICIT
     appearance (either one — Light is as affected as Dark) AppKit re-renders
     the TITLEBAR chrome wrong: items come out as blank pills, or drop out of
     the capture entirely. That looks exactly like a theming bug and is not
     one — on screen it is correct (user-confirmed 2026-08-06). Neither
     making the view's effectiveAppearance current for the draw nor reading
     the backing store with lockFocus + initWithFocusedViewRect: fixes it
     (the latter answers nil outright — layer-backed views do not support
     it). The window BODY is faithful either way, and the whole capture is
     faithful under #system. So: judge a forced-appearance toolbar by
     querying item state (toolbarItemNamed:, appearanceMenuItemFor:), and
     snapshot under #system when you need to SEE it."
    CocoaUI class >> snapshotTo: aPath [
        ^self snapshotWindow: Win to: aPath
    ]
    "Same capture for ANY window we own (the asset editors' gates snapshot
     their own windows through this)."
    CocoaUI class >> snapshotWindow: aWindow to: aPath [
        | view rect rep data |
        aWindow isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        view := (aWindow onMain contentView) onMain superview.
        rect := view onMain bounds.
        rep := view onMain bitmapImageRepForCachingDisplayInRect: rect.
        rep isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        view onMain cacheDisplayInRect: rect toBitmapImageRep: rep.
        "NSBitmapImageFileTypePNG = 4."
        data := rep onMain representationUsingType: 4
                    properties: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSDictionary') onMain dictionary).
        data isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        data onMain writeToFile: aPath atomically: false.
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> window [ ^Win ]
    CocoaUI class >> transcriptView [ ^Transcript ]
    "Scripting hooks (rusttcl `gui`) — the toolbar item for an identifier,
     for a test that wants to assert on its state. The A-/A+ actions
     themselves are just `CocoaUI increaseFont` / `decreaseFont`, since a
     native NSToolbarItem has no performClick:."
    "The view group's segments as a String — '#' the lit one, '.' the rest.
     Derived from selectedIndex, because NSToolbarItem exposes no
     per-subitem isSelected: under SelectOne the group's index IS the state.

     Which is also why the real invariant to assert on is
     viewGroupSelectionMode = 0. Passing 1 there is selectAny, under which
     every segment you click STAYS lit and the bar accumulates highlights —
     a bug this string cannot see, because AppKit keeps reporting a single
     selectedIndex the whole time."
    CocoaUI class >> viewSegmentStates [
        | group n idx out |
        group := self toolbarItemNamed: self viewGroupIdentifier.
        group isNil ifTrue: [ ^'' ].
        n := (group onMain subitems) sendI64: 'count'.
        idx := group sendI64: 'selectedIndex'.
        out := WriteStream on: String new.
        0 to: n - 1 do: [ :i | out nextPut: (i = idx ifTrue: [ $# ] ifFalse: [ $. ]) ].
        ^out contents
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> viewGroupSelectionMode [
        | group |
        group := self toolbarItemNamed: self viewGroupIdentifier.
        group isNil ifTrue: [ ^-1 ].
        ^group sendI64: 'selectionMode'
    ]

    CocoaUI class >> appearanceMenuItemFor: aSymbol [
        ThemeMenuItems isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        ^ThemeMenuItems at: aSymbol ifAbsent: [ nil ]
    ]
    CocoaUI class >> toolbarItemNamed: ident [
        ToolbarItems isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        ^ToolbarItems at: ident ifAbsent: [ nil ]
    ]
]
